Texy in Latte
*************

.[perex]
Do you write templates in [Latte |https://latte.nette.org]? Texy adds a `|texy` filter and a `{texy}` tag to them, so you can turn an article written in Markdown or Texy syntax into HTML right in the template, with all the typography and security Texy offers.


The |texy Filter
================

Our blog stores an article as text, and in the template we want to print it as HTML. First we tell Latte to use Texy. That's the job of `TexyExtension`, an extension that receives a `Texy\Texy` object:

```php
$texy = new Texy\Texy;

$latte = new Latte\Engine;
$latte->addExtension(new Texy\Bridges\Latte\TexyExtension($texy));
```

In the template, the `|texy` filter then converts the article:

```latte
<article>
	{$article->text|texy}
</article>
```

Normally Latte escapes every variable so that it can't turn into HTML. The `|texy` filter, however, returns finished HTML, so Latte prints it unchanged.

The extension follows the settings of the `$texy` object, so everything in [configuration |configuration] applies to templates as well.


Markdown
========

Do you write your articles in Markdown? Instead of a `Texy\Texy` object, pass the extension a function that converts the text:

```php
$markdown = new Texy\Markdown\Markdown;
$markdown->config->locale = 'en'; // English typography, the default

$latte->addExtension(new Texy\Bridges\Latte\TexyExtension($markdown->toHtml(...)));
```

Writing `$markdown->toHtml(...)` doesn't call the method; it hands the method to the extension as a function. Both the `|texy` filter and the `{texy}` tag then convert Markdown.


The {texy} Tag
==============

Sometimes the text sits right in the template, like the intro of a page or a footer. Write it between `{texy}` and `{/texy}`:

```latte
<aside>
	{texy}
	## How to Do It

	Dry them in a **thin** layer and turn them often.
	{/texy}
</aside>
```

With the Markdown extension from the previous section, this produces:

```html
<aside>
<h2>How to Do It</h2>

<p>Dry them in a <strong>thin</strong> layer and turn them often.</p>
</aside>
```

Before converting the text, the extension removes the indentation its lines share because of the surrounding template. That way Markdown doesn't read the indented lines as a code block.

Text that contains no variable is converted by Latte only once, when it compiles the template, so the compiled template already holds the finished HTML and never waits for Texy. When you change the settings of the `Texy\Texy` object, Latte notices and compiles the template again. If the extension received a function instead, for Markdown for example, it can't tell that the settings changed. In that case clear the Latte cache after the change, otherwise the template will keep printing HTML made with the old settings.

.[note]
A variable inside `{texy}` becomes part of the text Texy reads, including any HTML it contains. So never put text from visitors inside `{texy}`. It belongs in a filter with safe mode, as the next section shows.


Visitor Comments
================

The `|texy` filter is meant for your own texts, and Texy's default settings let almost any HTML through. For comments that anyone can write, use an object in [safe mode |configuration#Safe Mode]. Latte can have only one `|texy` filter, so for comments we add a filter of our own:

```php
$comments = new Texy\Markdown\Markdown;
$comments->config->locale = 'en'; // English typography, the default
Texy\Markdown\Configurator::safeMode($comments);

$latte->addFilter('comment', fn(string $text) => new Latte\Runtime\Html($comments->toHtml($text)));
```

```latte
<div class="comment">{$comment->text|comment}</div>
```

`Latte\Runtime\Html` tells Latte that the result is finished HTML and must not be escaped again. A script the author of a comment might try to slip in shows up as harmless text, and links get `rel="nofollow"`.

